><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• A moderate-intensity training run can in result loss of muscle&#xD;cell membrane integrity that may impair athlete recovery and&#xD;subsequent training performance.&#xD;• Elite endurance athletes in training may present with suboptimal&#xD;blood levels of vitamin E.&#xD;• This suggests elite athletes may benefit from personalized&#xD;nutritional supplementation&#xD;• Further research is suggested to examine if a critical level of α-&#xD;TOH is required to protect the muscle cell membrane in elite&#xD;male athletes.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
